# Erdos Problem #99 - empty-pentagon trapezoid endpoint

**Date:** 2026-09-14
**Target:** C-II, canonical full-core Type-II branch
**Dependencies:** AG42/Section-171 face floor; exact force-hull residual conservation; Wegner residual quantization; Groemer equality classification
**Claim class:** proof-only structural endpoint awaiting independent audit

## LEVEL I STATUS

`STILL OPEN`

Put

\[
X:=Q-f,
\qquad
\Delta_2={\pi\over2\sqrt3},
\qquad
f_*:=\sqrt{\Delta_2}-{\pi\over4}+0.00021
=0.167124643466509\ldots .
\tag{PT.1}
\]

This note identifies the simultaneous small-residual/small-degree-excess endpoint:

\[
\boxed{
R_F=o(D),\quad Q-f=o(D)
\Longrightarrow
\text{at least }(f_*-o(1))D\text{ exceptional pentagons are }o(1)
\text{-close to one flat unit trapezoid.}
}
\tag{PT.2}
\]

These pentagons have no assigned interior isolate and zero fixed-boundary spare
capacity. A pairwise vertex-disjoint subfamily has size at least

\[
\boxed{
\left({f_*\over21}-o(1)\right)D
=(0.007958316\ldots-o(1))D.
}
\tag{PT.3}
\]

Every member has one almost-flat boundary vertex and two hidden diagonals from that
vertex of length `1+o(1)`.

## 1. Classify the zero-residual empty pentagon

Let `P=(p_1,...,p_5)` be a convex equilateral unit-separated pentagon, allowing
collinearity in the closure, with no assigned interior source point. Its canonical
fixed-boundary residual is

\[
r(P)={A(P)\over a_0}-{3\over2},
\qquad a_0={\sqrt3\over2}.
\tag{PT.4}
\]

Assume `r(P)=0`. Then `A(P)=3sqrt(3)/4` and `per(P)=5`, so this is equality in the
scaled Oler/Wegner inequality. Hence the five points form a Groemer packing and admit
a triangulation by unit equilateral triangles. After one rigid motion they lie in one
unit triangular lattice.

Let the five oriented cyclic boundary edge vectors be `e_i=p_{i+1}-p_i`. Every `e_i`
is one of the six unit triangular-lattice directions and `sum e_i=0`. Let `n_j` count
the edges in direction `j*pi/3`, `j=0,...,5`, and put

\[
a=n_0-n_3,
\qquad b=n_1-n_4,
\qquad c=n_2-n_5.
\]

With `u_0=(1,0)`, `u_1=(1/2,sqrt(3)/2)`, and `u_2=u_1-u_0`, vector closure gives

\[
a u_0+b u_1+c u_2=0,
\]

hence

\[
a=c=-b=:t.
\tag{PT.5}
\]

Each opposite-direction pair sum has the same parity as its difference. The three pair
sums total five, so `t` is odd. They are each at least `|t|`, so `3|t|<=5` and
therefore `t=+/-1`.

For `t=1`, the counts are one copy of the 120-degree triple of directions `{0,2,4}`
plus opposite pairs. Since there are only five edges, there is exactly one opposite
pair. The `t=-1` case is the rotated complementary triple. Thus, up to rotation and
reflection, the direction multiset is

\[
\boxed{\{0,0,2\pi/3,\pi,4\pi/3\}.}
\tag{PT.6}
\]

Convex cyclic order forces this angular order. Integrating the five unit vectors gives

\[
(0,0),\ (1,0),\ (2,0),\
\left({3\over2},{\sqrt3\over2}\right),\
\left({1\over2},{\sqrt3\over2}\right).
\tag{PT.7}
\]

### Theorem PT.1 - unique empty-pentagon equality shape

Every zero-residual isolate-free unit equilateral pentagon is congruent, as a cyclic
five-point configuration, to (PT.7). It is the flat unit equilateral trapezoid with
long base two, short base one, and unit legs.

The middle point `(1,0)` on the long base has straight boundary angle `pi` and unit
hidden diagonals to both top vertices. The five boundary edge projective directions
have multiplicities `3,1,1` on three axes separated by `pi/3`.

## 2. Uniform stability toward the trapezoid

For every `epsilon>0` there is `tau_trap(epsilon)>0` such that every isolate-free
canonical pentagonal exceptional face with

\[
r(P)<\tau_{trap}(\epsilon)
\]

is `epsilon`-close, after a rigid motion, cyclic relabeling, and reflection, to (PT.7).

Indeed, otherwise a normalized counterexample sequence with residual tending to zero
would have a compact limit. The limit has residual zero and is congruent to (PT.7) by
Theorem PT.1, contradicting the fixed separation from its orbit.

## 3. The hard global endpoint is linearly pentagonal

Let `n_d` be the number of bounded exceptional faces of degree `d>=5`. Then

\[
X=Q-f=\sum_{d\ge6}(d-5)n_d,
\tag{PT.8}
\]

so the number of nonpentagonal exceptional faces obeys `N_{>=6}<=X`. On a subsequence
with `X=o(D)`, the AG42/Section-171 face floor gives

\[
p_5:=n_5=f-N_{\ge6}\ge(f_*-o(1))D.
\tag{PT.9}
\]

Assume also `R_F=o(D)`. Let

\[
\kappa={1\over\sqrt3}-{1\over2}>0
\]

be the first Wegner residual jump. For a pentagon, Wegner quantization gives

\[
r(P)<\kappa\quad\Longrightarrow\quad z(P)=0.
\tag{PT.10}
\]

The contact-wall capacity theorem then gives `M_partial(P)=z(P)=0`.

Fix `epsilon>0` and set

\[
\theta(\epsilon)=\min\{\kappa,\tau_{trap}(\epsilon)\}>0.
\]

All face residuals are nonnegative and sum to `R_F`, so at most
`R_F/theta(epsilon)=o(D)` pentagons have residual at least `theta(epsilon)`. Combining
with (PT.9), at least `(f_*-o(1))D` pentagons simultaneously have

1. no assigned interior isolate;
2. zero fixed-boundary spare capacity;
3. `epsilon`-closeness to the flat trapezoid (PT.7).

Because this holds for every fixed `epsilon`, a standard diagonal choice yields
`epsilon_D->0` while retaining the same first-order count. This proves (PT.2).

## 4. Extract linearly many vertex-disjoint pentagonal teeth

Build the conflict graph on the good pentagons, joining two when their boundary vertex
sets meet. A triangle-free unit-contact graph has contact degree at most five. Thus a
fixed boundary vertex belongs to at most five distinct bounded faces. A pentagon has
five boundary vertices, and through each vertex at most four other selected pentagons
can conflict with it. Hence

\[
\Delta(G_{face})\le20.
\tag{PT.11}
\]

A greedy independent set keeps at least one twenty-first of the good pentagons. This
proves (PT.3).

## 5. Exact geometric normal form

For every extracted pentagon label the almost-flat vertex `m`, the two adjacent
long-base endpoints `l,r`, and the two opposite top vertices `a,b` according to the
nearest copy of (PT.7). The five boundary sides remain exact unit contacts, while

\[
|m-a|=1+o(1),
\qquad
|m-b|=1+o(1),
\tag{PT.12}
\]

with both distances strictly greater than one in every actual triangle-free source,
and

\[
\angle lmr=\pi-o(1).
\tag{PT.13}
\]

The five boundary terminal directions collapse to three triangular-lattice projective
axes with multiplicities `3,1,1`.

Thus the simultaneous small-`R_F`, small-`Q-f` corner has a concrete local model:

```text
Omega(D) empty pentagonal holes,
  -> fixed-boundary capacity exactly saturated,
  -> each hole is an o(1) perturbation of one three-triangle lattice trapezoid,
  -> one almost-flat contact-chain vertex,
  -> two missing triangle edges at length 1+o(1),
  -> three local train-direction axes with multiplicities 3,1,1.
```

This is stronger than the generic anchored-Groemer conclusion because the third
near-triangle vertices are positive-core boundary vertices, not assigned contact
isolates, and the entire five-point face shape is classified.

## 6. What remains

This theorem does not turn either hidden `1+o(1)` diagonal into an exact contact.
Contact minimality does not forbid adding contacts at fixed diameter, near-active
constraints do not automatically receive positive KKT multipliers, and fixed-wall
cardinality saturation is not local rigidity.

The next source-level gate is specific: prove that a linear vertex-disjoint family of
these near-flat, externally braced pentagonal teeth cannot coexist with the full-core
KKT/diameter framework unless one hidden diagonal activates exactly or there is a
same-cardinality strict diameter decrease.

C-II and Level I remain open.
